## ABSTRACT
Developing efficient and cost-effective electrode materials is of essential significance to advance various energy storage technologies, among which flexible supercapacitors hold great promise to meet the growing popularity of wearable electronics. Herein, we report a homologous strategy to parallelly synthesize phosphorus-doped \( \mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) (P-ZnCo\(_{2}\)O\(_{4}\)@NCC) and nitrogen-doped carbon (NC@NCC) both derived from ZnCo-metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) precursors in-situ grown on dopamine-modified carbon cloth (NCC) as conductive substrates. Impressively, the as-obtained P-ZnCo\(_{2}\)O\(_{4}\)@NCC can achieve a high specific capacitance of \( 2702.2\ \mathrm{mF\cdot cm^{-2}} \) at \( 1\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm^{-2}} \) with the capacitance retention rate exceeding \( 75.3\% \) at \( 10\ \mathrm{mA\cdot cm^{-2}} \), demonstrating the outstanding rate capability. Moreover, flexible solid-state hybrid supercapacitors, using P-ZnCo\(_{2}\)O\(_{4}\)@NCC as positive electrode and NC@NCC as negative electrode, are assembled with poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA)/KOH as the gel electrolyte, which deliver the energy density of \( 11.9\ \mathrm{mWh\cdot cm^{-3}} \) when the power density reaches up to \( 47.3\ \mathrm{mW\cdot cm^{-3}} \). In addition, \( 85.15\% \) of the initial specific capacitance is maintained after 5000 continuous cycles and no obvious capacitance decay is observed under different bending conditions, revealing the excellent cycling stability and flexibility. As a proof-of-concept demonstration, two assembled hybrid supercapacitors connected in series can light up a red light-emitting diode (LED) under the bending angle of \( 180^{\circ} \), heralding the feasibility for broad practical applications.
## KEYWORDS
homologous synthesis, heteroatom-doping, metal-organic frameworks, energy storage, flexible solid-state devices
## 1 Introduction
The emergence of portable and wearable electronics, such as real-time healthcare monitoring devices, increasingly demands the advent of advanced energy storage systems (EES) with both high energy density and good compatibility/flexibility, which could potentially revolutionize our future lifestyles [1–3]. Flexible supercapacitors, as one of the most promising sustainable EES, have recently triggered tremendous attention due to their foldability, light weight, high power density, and high-rate capability [4–6]. Their unsatisfactory energy density, compared with alternative counterparts of lithium-ion batteries, however, inevitably impedes the large-scale adoption and deployment in consumable electronics. Recent investigations have revealed that the construction of hybrid supercapacitors assembled with positive battery-type Faradaic electrode and negative capacitive electrode could be an effective pathway to address the above-mentioned issues, which can expand the voltage range and achieve desirable energy density without sacrificing intrinsically high-power density [7–9]. The battery-type material \( \mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) for instance, has received much attention towards hybrid supercapacitors due to its environmental friendliness, rich redox features, and high theoretical specific capacity (890 mAh.g\(^{-2}\)) [10–12]. However, the poor conductivity, sluggish charge transfer kinetics, low-rate capability, and cycling stability collectively limit the further exploitation as advanced electrodes. One of the alternative and effective routes is coupling with two different transition metals to improve the specific capacitance and cycling stability of pure \( \mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4} \) due to synergistic effects, which have been widely reported with varied success [13–15].
