Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted extensive research interests ranging from batteries, solar cells, and supercapacitors to water splitting, benefiting from their ease of synthesis, diverse structural morphologies, adjustable pore sizes, large specific surface areas, and their ability to form structures through the connection of rich central metal ions and organic ligands, which can provide sufficient space for energy and environmental applications [16–19]. However, for electrochemical applications, the low conductivity and large resistances of MOFs undoubtedly call for a new strategy, necessitating the exploration to enhance their electrochemical performances. Typically, MOFs are formed through intermolecular bonding or coordination bonding between metal ions or clusters, and the organic linking groups offer unique two-dimensional (2D) or three-dimensional (3D) structural frameworks [20–24]. Among various MOFs,
zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are one of the most widely investigated, which can offer advantageous metal ion coordination based on imidazole skeleton and imidazole ligand. In addition, the abundance in nitrogen, carbon, and metal ions is conducive to electrolyte ion diffusion pathways, thereby improving the conductivity [25–27]. Among the reported ZIFs with various metal ions, zinc/cobalt-based ZIFs exhibit remarkable binary reversible redox pairs and extremely high stability in high concentration alkaline electrolyte media [28–31].
Recently, metal phosphide-based electrodes have shown excellent conductivity and are expected to demonstrate huge potential in energy storage [32–35]. Among these electrode materials, phosphorus (P)-doped MOFs have been enormously investigated to enhance the reaction activity by fundamentally tuning the electronic and surface structures through surface engineering in various aspects such as electrocatalysis, electronics, and photonics [36–40]. Specifically, a groundbreaking work by Xia et al. reported that phosphite ions doped \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) can significantly improve the surface reaction characteristics and electrode kinetics through surface functionalization. Inspired by the anion doping strategy, P-doped transition metal oxides also possess properties of increasing electron carriers and electroactive sites, which helps improve the dynamics of electron conduction and ion transport, thus enhancing the electrochemical performance of these materials [41]. Furthermore, Sun's group reported that P-doped \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) increased the electron density near the Fermi level and reduced the reaction free energy, and had more charge carriers and lower energy barriers, thus improving the resulting electrocatalytic performances [42].
In this work, dopamine-modified carbon cloth (NCC) was utilized as conductive substrates to in-situ grow ZnCo-MOFs precursor, and subsequently positive and negative electrodes were prepared through separate processing pathways. Specifically, \(\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) was formed via carbonization of ZnCo-MOFs followed by oxidation, and then phosphorus doping was performed to obtain the positive electrode \((\mathrm{P-ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NCC})\), which achieved a specific capacitance of \(2702.2\,\mathrm{mF\cdot cm^{-2}}\) at \(1\,\mathrm{mA\cdot cm^{-2}}\) with the capacitance retention rate exceeding \(70.6\%\) at \(10\,\mathrm{mA\cdot cm^{-2}}\). Moreover, after carbonization of ZnCo-MOFs, acid etching was carried out to form nitrogen-doped carbon \((\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC})\), which was used as the negative electrode. Finally, flexible solid-state hybrid supercapacitors were assembled with poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA)/KOH as the gel electrolyte, which delivered the energy density of \(11.9\,\mathrm{mWh\cdot cm^{-3}}\) when the power density reached \(47.3\,\mathrm{mW\cdot cm^{-3}}\). After 5000 continuous cycles, the specific capacitance was still maintained above \(85.15\%\), showing good cycling stability. In addition, the device shows excellent flexibility, and no obvious capacitance decay is observed under different bending conditions. Two as-assembled hybrid supercapacitors connected in series can light up a red light-emitting diode (LED), demonstrating the feasibility for practical applications.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Synthesis of positive and negative electrode materials
Figure 1(a) illustrates the preparation processes of P\(\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NCC}\) and \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}\) via the proposed homologous strategy, which are employed as positive and negative electrodes for the flexible solid-state hybrid supercapacitor, respectively. Specifically, ZnCo-MOFs were firstly in-situ grown on NCC as flexible conductive substrate at room temperature. Subsequently, two separate and parallel pathways were adopted as follows: On the one hand, \(\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) was formed via carbonization of ZnCo
MOFs followed by oxidation, and then phosphorus doping (phosphorization) was performed to obtain the positive electrode materials \((\mathrm{P-ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})\). On the other hand, after pyrolysis of ZnCo-MOFs, acid etching was carried out to form NC as the negative electrode material.
## 2.2 Material characterizations of \(\mathbf{P-ZnCo}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{4}@\mathbf{NCC}\) and NC@NCC
Eventually, the \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NCC}\) was formed through the phosphatization process and no significant change in surface morphology was observed (Fig. 1(d) and Fig. S6 in the ESM). To further examine the elemental distributions in \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NCC}\), elemental mapping was performed. It was found that phosphorus is distributed across the entire carbon cloth and doped into \(\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) as well as the carbon layer, and the presence of Zn, Co, N, O, and P elements is observed (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The SEM images of \(\mathrm{ZnCo-MOFs}@\mathrm{NCC}\) annealed at \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{h}\) under argon atmosphere at different magnifications are presented in Figs. S8(a) and S8(b) in the ESM. It can be observed that the ZnCo-MOFs are well loaded onto the modified carbon cloth surface, and a thick carbon layer is formed on the surface of the carbon cloth. After refluxing in \(3\,\mathrm{M}\,\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\) overnight, \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}\) electrode material is obtained. As shown in Figs. S9(a) and S9(b) in the ESM, the MOF particles on the carbon cloth surface have disappeared, which can be ascribed to continuous stirring and falling off in the solution, but the carbon layer on the surface is still attached to the carbon cloth. This structure is beneficial for the full contact with the electrolyte and can be used as a negative electrode for supercapacitors [43,44].
To further analyze the atomic structure of \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NCC}\), transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) characterizations were performed. As shown in Fig. 1(e) and Fig. S10(a) in the ESM, the TEM images of \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) at different magnifications reveal that \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) maintains a dodecahedral structure with particle sizes of \(200-300\,\mathrm{nm}\). Figures 1(f) and 1(g) show the HRTEM images of \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NCC}\), and lattice fringes with spacings of \(0.294\) and \(0.243\,\mathrm{nm}\) correspond to the (220) and (311) crystal planes of \(\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\), respectively [45]. It should be noted that the lattice fringe spacing of \(\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) assigned to the (311) crystal plane is reported to be \(0.238\,\mathrm{nm}\) [46]. Due to the local lattice distortion caused by the substitution of O by P, the as-synthesized sample has a slightly larger ionic radius. The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (Fig. S10(b) in the ESM) presented the diffraction rings corresponding to the (101), (440), (511), and (642) crystal planes of \(\mathrm{ZnCo}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) [47,48]. The high-angle annular dark field (HAADF) images and elemental mapping (Fig. 1(h) and
Fig. S10(c) in the ESM) show that the \(Z\mathfrak{n}\) , Co, P, O, and N elements are uniformly distributed in \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}},\) further confirming the successfully doping of P into the material.
