## 2.3 Electrochemical performances of \(\mathbf{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) and NC@NCC
The electrochemical performances of as-prepared electrode materials were first evaluated in the three-electrode system, where \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}\) were used as reference and counter electrodes, respectively. Figure 3(a) shows the cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\), \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@CC}\), \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\), and \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@CC}\) positive electrodes at a scanning rate of \(5\ \mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\), demonstrating that these electrodes exhibit characteristic battery-type redox peaks. And the peak currents of \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) are higher than those of other electrodes, indicating that P doping and dopamine modification can greatly enhance the electrochemical activity of \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\). Furthermore, the electrical resistances of these four electrodes were evaluated by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) in the frequency range from \(10^{-2}\) to \(10^{5}\,\mathrm{Hz}\). In the Nyquist plot, the intrinsic ohmic resistance of \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) is the lowest (Fig. S12 in the ESM), and the slope of linear portion of the \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) electrode in the high frequency region is larger than those of other three electrodes, suggesting the fast charge transfer. High-content N-doping derived from the dopamine and ZnCo-MOFs precursors increases the ion-accessible surface area, thereby facilitating the ion diffusion and achieving better electrode/electrolyte contact interface. In addition, P-doping enhances the electrical conductivity of \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) [53], thus providing faster transport pathways of electrons and faster ions diffusion at the electrode/electrolyte interface.
Galvanostatic charging/discharging (GCD) in Fig. 3(b) also reveals the highest capacitance of the \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) electrode when compared with the other three electrodes at a current density of \(1\,\mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\), which is consistent with the CV results shown in Fig. 3(a), indicating that P-doping significantly enhanced the electrochemical performance. Figure 3(c) shows the GCD curves of \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) electrode at various current densities. Based on the GCD curves, the areal capacitances are calculated to be 2702.2, 2506.2, 2296.9, 2137.3, 2007.1, and \(1906.7\ \mathrm{mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\) at current densities of 1, 2, 4, 6 A, and \(10\;\mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\), respectively (Fig. 3(d)). Obviously, when the current density increases by 10 times, \(70.6\%\) of capacitance delivered at \(1\;\mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\) can be retained, demonstrating the excellent rate performance of the \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) electrode. Figure 3(e) shows the CV curves of the \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) electrode at different scan rates, which is helpful for better understanding the reaction kinetics of the \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) electrode. The CV curves indicate that the entire electrochemical storage reaction includes diffusion-controlled and surface-controlled processes. In general, the relationship between diffusion/capacitive contributions to current is analyzed based on Eq. (1) [54]
3(d)). Obviously, when the current density increases by 10 times, \(70.6\%\) of capacitance delivered at \(1\;\mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\) can be retained, demonstrating the excellent rate performance of the \(\mathrm{P-}\) \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C}\) electrode. Figure 3(e) shows the CV curves of the \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C}\) electrode at different scan rates, which is helpful for better understanding the reaction kinetics of the \(\mathrm{P-}\) \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C}\) electrode. The CV curves indicate that the entire electrochemical storage reaction includes diffusion-controlled and surface-controlled processes. In general, the relationship between diffusion/capacitive contributions to current is analyzed based on Eq. (1) [54]
\[
I=a\nu^{b}
\]
where \(i\) represents the current, \(a\) is a constant, \(\nu\) is the sweep rate, and \(b\) is the slope value of the fitted line. For absolute diffusion-controlled and capacitive-controlled processes, the \(b\) values are 0.5 and 1.0, respectively. When the \(b\) value ranges from 0.5 to 1.0, the material exhibits both battery and capacitive behaviors. The plot of \(\log(i)\) vs. \(\log(\nu)\) indicates the average \(b\) value of \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) electrode is 0.65 (Fig. 3(f)), suggesting there exist both capacitive-controlled and diffusion-controlled behaviors. And the ratio of capacitive and diffusion contributions can be quantified by using CV results and Dunn's equation (Eq. (2)) [55]
\[
i(V)=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}v^{1/2}
\]
where \(i(V)\) denotes the current at a specific voltage \(V\) (mA), \(\nu\) represents sweep speed \((\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1})\), and \(k_{1}\) and \(k_{2}\) are constants.
As shown in Fig. 3(g), the capacitive contribution increases with scanning rate, which can be ascribed to the rapid charge storage rate. \(47.4\%\) capacitive contribution at \(30\ \mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\) can be achieved for \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) electrode, as illustrated in Fig. 3(h). Cycling stability is one of the essential indicators for evaluating an electrode material. Figure 3(i) shows the cycling stability performances of \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) and \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) electrodes at a current density of \(3\ \mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\). And the \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) electrode still delivers \(93.2\%\) initial capacitance after 5000 cycles, which is much higher than the \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC}\) (73.8% after 5000 cycles), implying the P-doping provides sufficient active sites to store charge, reduces internal resistance, and establishes stable electrode/electrolyte interface, thereby
significantly enhancing the electrochemical performance of \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C}\).
Parallelly, \(\mathrm{ZnCo-MOF_{8}@N C C}\) samples were annealed at 400, 600, 800, and \(1000\ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) followed by pickling process to obtain optimal negative electrodes (denoted as \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}400,\) \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}600\) , \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) , and \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}1000)\) to pair with the \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C}\) positive electrodes. Figure 4(a) displays the CV curves of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}400\) , \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}600\) , \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) , and \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}1000\) negative electrodes at a scan rate of \(5\,\mathrm{\mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\) , and each sample shows a nearly rectangular shape, indicating typical electrical double layer capacitance (EDLC) characteristic. And the rectangular areas of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}\) 800 and \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}1000\) electrodes are almost coincided, higher than the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}400\) and \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}600\) electrodes, indicating that the relatively higher degree of graphitization is expected to achieve better conductivity favorable for charge storage.
Figure 4(b) shows the GCD profiles of these four negative electrodes, and there is almost complete coincidence between \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) and \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}1000\) electrodes at \(1\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) . The Nyquist plots further reveal that the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) electrode has higher electrical conductivity than \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}1000\) (Fig. S13 in the ESM). Therefore, the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) is chosen as optimal negative electrode. GCD curves of the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) electrode show high symmetry and nearly linear slopes at 1, 2, 4, 6, 8, and \(10\ \mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\) (Fig. 4(c)), and the corresponding areal capacitances are 1572.2, 1422.6, 1328.3, 1284.6, 1240.4, and \(1230.2\,\mathrm{\mF{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\) , respectively. Even if the current density increases by 10 times, it still achieves \(78.2\%\) capacitance retention, showing excellent rate performance. Figure 4(d) further compares the rate areal capacitance of these four negative electrodes, and the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}\)
800 exhibits the highest rate areal capacitance, especially at high current densities, which can be ascribed to its better conductivity and compatible graphitization degree. Figure 4(e) shows the CV curves of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) at various scan rates. Moreover, the \(b\) value for the anodic current is 0.9 (Fig. 4(f)), demonstrating the faster capacitive-controlled charge storage.
Figure 4(g) depicts the ratio of capacitive to diffusive contribution at various scan rates. The capacitive contribution is \(68.4\%\) at a slow scanning rate of \(5\,\mathrm{\mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\) , while the capacitive contribution increases with increasing scan rate, suggesting the fast charging/discharging storage rate. As depicted in Fig. 4(h), the capacitive contribution reaches \(77.1\%\) at a high scanning rate of \(30\ \mathrm{mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\) , suggesting fast ion storage rate at high current density. Figure 4(i) shows the cyclic stability of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) and \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}1000\) electrodes at \(8\ \mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\) , and the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) retains \(88.2\%\) capacitance retention after 5000 cycles, which is much higher than \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}1000\) ( \(76.3\%\) after 5000 cycles). These results clearly annotate the remarkable capacitance, cycling, and rate performance of \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) electrode, which benefits from the high electrical conductivity and appropriate degree of graphitization.
2.4 Electrochemical performances of \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C//N C@N C C}\) flexible solid-state hybrid supercapacitors
Asymmetric flexible solid-state hybrid supercapacitors (ASCs) were prepared by using \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C}\) as positive electrode, \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) as negative electrode, and PVA/KOH as gel electrolyte (Fig. 5(a)). Based on the testing results in three-electrode systems, \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C}\) and \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) electrodes
exhibit areal capacitances of 2702 and \(1572\;\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) at \(1\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) over different potential windows, respectively (Fig. 5(b)), suggesting the as-assembled ASCs should have a stable potential window of \(1.65\,\mathrm{~V~}\) . To further achieve high energy and power densities, the mass ratio of \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C}\) positive electrode to \(\mathrm{NC@NCC-800}\) negative electrode was set as 1.29 to balance the positive and negative capacitances. Figure 5(c) shows the CV curves of \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C/N C@N C C-800}\) ASCs with different cut-off potentials from 1.4 to \(2.0\;\mathrm{V}\) . However, the cathodic current becomes noticeable when the cut-off window exceeds \(1.8\,\mathrm{V}\) . Thus, the electrochemical performances of these \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C/N C@N C C-800}\) ASCs are measured in the potential window of 0–1.6 V. The CV curves of the \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C/N C@N C C-800}\) ASC at various scan rates are shown in Fig. 5(d), from which both the EDLC and pseudocapacitive behaviors can be observed, and the shape of curves is maintained with the increase of scan rates.
Figure 5(e) presents the GCD curves of the \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C/N C@N C C-800}\) ASC at various current densities from 2 to \(20\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) , and no obvious \(i R\) drop can be observed when the current density increased by 10 times, suggesting the fast charge/discharge kinetics for the \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C/N C@N C C-800}\) ASC. Calculated from the GCD curves, the corresponding areal capacities are 1107.5, 1047.5, 949.5, 880.5, 825.1 and \(783.8\;\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) at current densities of 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 and \(20\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) , respectively (Fig. 5(f)). Remarkably, the \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C/N C@N C C-800}\) ASC rendered \(70.2\%\) initial capacitance retention at a high current density of \(20\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) , demonstrating that the \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C/N C@N C C-800}\) ASC has the potential to be utilized for fast energy storage devices. For comparison, previous
results on flexible solid state supercapacitor are listed in Table S1 in the ESM.
Cycling stability is one of the critical issues in flexible energy storage devices for practical application. The flexible solid-state \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C/N C@N C C-800}\) ASC delivers an ultrahigh stability of \(85.15\%\) after 5000 cycles, as presented in Fig. 5(g). The Ragone plot correlating the power densities \((P,\,\mathrm{mWh}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\) with the areal energy densities \((E,\,\mathrm{mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\) depicts the highest power density of \(479.7\;\mathrm{mWh}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) at an energy density of \(8.4\;\mathrm{mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) , which is much higher than previous reported results (Fig. 5(h)) [56–65]. Flexibility is one of crucial criteria for future portable and wearable energy storage devices. Figure S1 in the ESM shows the CV curves under various blending angles, and no obvious change in shapes and capacitances can be observed, demonstrating excellent flexibility and mechanical stability under different bending conditions. The corresponding optical graphs of pouch-type structure \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C/N C@N C C-800}\) ASC in different bending conditions are shown in Fig. 5(i). Glowing a red LED using two \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C/N C@N C C-800}\) ASC devices assembled in series under flat and bending conditions indicates the practical viability of future portable and wearable energy storage devices (Fig. 5(i)).
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, a novel homologous strategy is developed to parallelly synthesize both positive \((\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C})\) and negative \((\mathrm{NC@NCC-800})\) electrodes for flexible hybrid supercapacitors. The as-obtained \(\mathrm{P-ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@N C C}\) can achieve a high specific capacitance of \(2702.2\;\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) at \(1\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) with the
capacitance retention rate exceeding \(70.6\%\) at \(10~\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m}^{-2}\) , demonstrating the outstanding rate capability. Similarly, the \(\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{NCC}{-}800\) negative electrode delivers high capacitive performance \((1572~\mathrm{\mF{\cdot}c m}^{-2}\) at \(1\ \mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m}^{-2})\) , enhanced ratecapability \((1230.1\ \mathrm{mF{\cdot}c m}^{-2}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) , along with exceptional cyclic stability \((88.2\%\) capacitance retention after 5000 cycles). Moreover, flexible solid-state P- \(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@NCC//N C@NCC-800}\) ASCs deliver high areal capacitance of \(1107.5~\mathrm{\mF{\cdot}c m}^{-2}\) at \(2\ \mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m}^{-2}\) and \(85.15\%\) capacitance retention after 5000 continuous cycles. The device features a high power density of \(479.7\,\mathrm{\mWh{\cdot}c m}^{-3}\) at an energy density of \(8.4\,\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m}^{-3}\) , and no obvious capacitance decay is observed under different bending conditions, revealing the excellent cycling stability and flexibility. As a proof-of-concept demonstration, two as-assembled hybrid supercapacitors connected in series can light up a red LED under the bending angle of \(180^{\circ}\) , heralding the feasibility for practical
applications.
